timestamp=2026-11-09T08:33:40.388Z level=INFO run=d54a47dc message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Check another case: \"124)\" was in our test earlier\ns = \"124)\"\n# rule says \"enclosed in parentheses\" - both present. But our old test had this. Maybe treat as negative if starts with '(' or ends with ')' or both? Or just contains '(' and ')'? Or contains '(' OR ')' alone? Or more precisely, if the number is enclosed in parens. But in messy data, maybe parens are partial. Let us also see the original sample: \"113 , 61 C2) \" had ) and it gave us 113.61 as positive in our earlier interpretation? Wait look back at one of our earlier test results - when we had \"113 , 61 C2) \" with our first naive logic with \"if '(' in s and ')' in s: is_neg=True\", in that case we got is_neg True. But also had \"-\" causing neg? No. But combined with other processing - let us look at our final corrected parser behavior on that specific case.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
