timestamp=2026-11-09T08:37:16.289Z level=INFO run=d54a47dc message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport re\ns = '-74,06C2'\n# Rule 4: \"Remove tokens (\\\"C1\\\" and \\\"C2\\\"), and strip out all letters and spaces, keeping only digits and the characters \\\",\\\" and \\\".\\\".\"\n# So first remove tokens C1 and C2 (whole tokens?). Also in \"06C2\", is C2 a token? Or just characters? If it’s attached to digits, is it treated as a token? The example \"80,87 C2\" becomes ... after removing token C2, and stripping letters/spaces. In practice, C2 appears as a separate token sometimes (space before) or sometimes attached like \"06C2\". As a token, usually space-delimited or standalone. But in the data it appears in both forms. The rule says \"tokens (\\\"C1\\\" and \\\"C2\\\")\" - so remove those specific tokens regardless of position? That’s ambiguous. But in attached form like \"74,06C2\", removing the token C2 as a substring might be intended. Let us also look at other examples: \"138.87 C1\" → C1 is separate; \"515.34\" has no C1/C2; \"1.234,56 C2\" has C2 attached in some cases? \"1.234,56 C2\" appears - in the sample earlier \"1.234,56 C2\" as the full amount string had C2 possibly attached or space? Wait look at one: \"R00017,2025-09-06T20:15:59Z,A6,web,96 C2,flag=T; adj=+15.50%; items=10; fee=+35.43\" - space before C2. Another: \"R00087,2025-09-12T18:39:00Z,A7,app,12.345 C2,...\" - \"12.345 C2\" (dot as decimal? 345 with 3 digits after dot - by rule 6, 3 digits after dot means dot is NOT decimal, it’s thousands separator → so \"12.345\" becomes 12345. Also C2 as token). Another: \"97,20 C2\" has C2 separate → becomes 97.20. All correct with removing C2 as token. But when it’s \"74,06C2\" (no space), is C2 still a token here? Or should we just remove the characters 'C' and '2'? That gives the right numeric part \"74,06\". The rule also says \"strip out all letters and spaces\" - that would remove C and 2 as letters. So maybe do both: remove tokens (C1, C2) and also strip letters/spaces. Or interpret \"remove tokens...\" as remove the specific token strings (word boundary), and separately strip other letters. In the attached case, C2 is not a separate token by word boundary; so word-boundary removal doesn’t catch it, but \"strip out all letters\" catches it. That matches. So the order is: (1) trim, (2) handle empty/negatives detection, (3) remove tokens C1/C2 (maybe as substrings in a specific form? or with word boundaries), (4) strip letters and spaces, (5) keep only digits , and ., etc. The rules as written: step 4 says \"Remove tokens (\\\"C1\\\" and \\\"C2\\\"), and strip out all letters and spaces...\" - so do both operations. Tokens might be removed regardless of word boundaries? Or as specified. Let us look at how it behaves on the attached case.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
