timestamp=2026-11-09T08:37:43.901Z level=INFO run=d54a47dc message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# For \"C1 1.000\", is 1000.0 the correct interpretation? That depends on locale. In many European formats, 1.000 means 1 (thousand separator) - so 1.000 is 1. In US format, 1.000 is 1.000. But rule 6: \"if only one type of separator exists, treat it as a decimal separator only when exactly two digits follow its last occurrence\"\n# For \"1.000\": dot is the only separator, last dot is at pos (1.000), digits after last dot: \"000\" is 3 digits. So exactly 2 digits? No, 3 digits. Therefore treat it as NOT decimal → so it’s a thousands/grouping separator? Then we remove it → \"1.000\" becomes \"1000\" if we just remove dots as thousands. But that gives 1000.0. That can’t be right if it’s meant as 1. Or if it’s meant as 1000.\n# But also \"100.00\": dot is decimal separator here? Because 2 digits follow → so dot IS decimal. \"100.00\" becomes 100.00 → 100.0. Correct.\n# Another: \"C1 1.000\" from the data row R00097 had amount \"C1 1.000\" and fee/items/adj given. Let us look at that row’s full values to infer the intended numeric scale.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
