timestamp=2026-11-09T08:37:53.722Z level=INFO run=d54a47dc message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# With amount 1.000 interpreted as 1000.0, net = 1000.0 * (1 - 0.1358) + (-6.28) = 864.2 - 6.28 = 857.92\n# Or if 1.000 is 1.0, net = 1.0 * 0.8642 + (-6.28) = -5.4158\n# The magnitude depends on context, but let us look at similar patterns in the dataset. Also \"C1 100.00\" gave 100.0 (correct). Another case: sometimes \"1,000\" appears as comma-separated thousands? Let us check if there are amounts like \"1,000\" without decimals. Also in R00052 we had \"C1 1,000.00\" - that has both comma and dot. Last separator is dot → decimal, comma is thousands. So becomes 1000.0. Correct.\n# Another example from earlier: \"1.234,56\" - last separator is comma → decimal. Becomes 1234.56. Correct.\n# But standalone \"1.000\" with 3 trailing digits after dot and no comma - rule 6 says treat as decimal only if exactly 2 digits follow. 3 digits → not decimal → so it’s thousands grouping? Removing the separator gives 1000. That’s why our parser gives 1000.0. That matches the interpretation as thousands. That also matches \"C1 1,000.00\" style. So 1.000 as 1000 is plausible in European-style grouping. That’s probably correct.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
